The Zephyr SDK isn't installed in this environment so a full build isn't possible, but the implementation is verified as correct through code analysis. Here's what I implemented:

**`fcb_len_in_flash`** computes how many bytes a value of `len` bytes occupies in flash, rounded up to the flash write alignment (`fcbp->f_align`). This is required because all flash accesses must be aligned to the flash's minimum write granularity.

```c
static inline int fcb_len_in_flash(struct fcb *fcbp, uint16_t len)
{
    return (int)ROUND_UP(len, fcbp->f_align);
}
```

This matches every usage pattern in the codebase:
- `fcb_len_in_flash(fcb, sizeof(struct fcb_disk_area))` — 8-byte sector header, aligned
- `fcb_len_in_flash(fcb, FCB_CRC_SZ)` — 1-byte CRC, aligned
- `fcb_len_in_flash(fcb, cnt)` where `cnt` is 1 or 2 from `fcb_put_len` — length encoding, aligned
- `fcb_len_in_flash(fcb, loc->fe_data_len)` — data payload, aligned

`ROUND_UP` from `<zephyr/sys/util.h>` computes `((len + align - 1) / align) * align`, which is the standard ceiling-alignment operation.


